      \field{abstract}{{QBoost}, a quantum-inspired ensemble learning algorithm, has performance that depends on the quality of weak classifiers and efficiency of the optimization process. Existing methods suffer from insufficient diversity among weak classifiers and low efficiency in handling nonlinear data patterns, leading to unsatisfactory generalization ability. This study proposes a new weak classifier construction method based on pruned random decision trees-combined with a multi-objective optimized {QBoost} model-to fix these problems. Weak classifiers are constructed using random subspaces with adaptive tree pruning; this enhances diversity while cutting overfitting. For {QBoost}, the study introduces a multi-objective optimization framework to minimize classification loss and maximize weak classifier diversity at the same time, and uses an improved {AdamW} algorithm to speed up convergence. Experiments on {KDD} Cup 99, {IMDb}, and Covertype datasets show the proposed method hits 96.8 \% classification accuracy on {KDD} Cup 99- {\textbackslash}mathbf3. 3 \% higher than Gradient Boosting (93.5 \%) and 2.6 \% higher than standard {QBoost} (94.2 \%). Compared with standard {QBoost}, its training time drops by 37 \%, and it keeps 89.7 \% accuracy under 15 \% feature noise-proving better robustness. These findings confirm the proposed method effectively improves classification performance, efficiency, and diversity.}
